sec.p:
Theta oscillations were obtained from CA1 LFPs filtered at 6–12 Hz using a zero-phase filter in MATLAB (bandpass IIR filter; filter order = 8). Theta phase was estimated by Hilbert transformation of the filtered signal. For each neuron, spike times occurring during selected theta epochs were matched to the nearest LFP sample, and the corresponding instantaneous theta phase was extracted for circular statistical analysis. Phase locking was quantified using CircStat functions (Berens, 2009). We used standard Rayleigh test to determine uniformity of phase distribution (Totah et al., 2013), in which the p value for circular uniformity is calculated as follows:
p=exp⁡(sqrt(1+4∗n+4∗(n2−R2))−(1+2∗n)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle p = \exp \left(sqrt \left( 1 + 4 * n + 4 * \left(n^2 - R^2 \right) \right) - \left( 1 + 2 * n \right) \right)$$\end{document}
and Rayleigh’s z to determine the strength of spike–LFP phase locking as follows:
z=R2/n \begin{document}$$\displaystyle z=R^{2} / n$$\end{document}
where R is the sum of resultant vectors across n spikes. Log-transformed Rayleigh z values were calculated log z values and stored for each neuron/group.
